{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for you for a keeping.</b> This is a term of inspection, for it requires inspection for a blemish four days before slaughter (Pesachim 96). And why did He advance its taking before its slaughter by four days, which He did not command thus for the Passover of all generations? Rabbi Mattya ben Ḥarash would say: Behold, it says ״וָאֶעֱבֹר עָלַיִךְ וָאֶרְאֵךְ וְהִנֵּה עִתֵּךְ עֵת דּוֹדִים״ (Ezekiel 16) — the oath had arrived that I swore to Abraham that I would redeem his children, and they had in their hand no commandments to engage in so that they might be redeemed, as it says ״וְאַתְּ עֵרוֹם וְעֶרְיָה״ (ibid.), and He gave them two commandments — the blood of Passover and the blood of circumcision — for they circumcised on that night, as it says ״מִתְבּוֹסֶסֶת בְּדָמָיִךְ״ (ibid.) — in two bloods, and it says ״גַּם אַתְּ בְּדַם בְּרִיתֵךְ שִׁלַּחְתִּי אֲסִירַיִךְ מִבּוֹר אֵין מַיִם בּוֹ״ (Zechariah 9); and because they were steeped in idolatry, He said to them <b>Draw out and take for yourselves</b> — draw your hands away from idolatry, and take for yourselves a sheep of a commandment (Mekhilta)." "<b>And you shall slaughter it, etc.</b> Do all of them slaughter? Rather, from here: a person's agent is like himself (Kiddushin 41)." "<b>The whole assembly of the congregation of Israel.</b> קָהָל and עֵדָה and יִשְׂרָאֵל; from here they said: the communal Passover offerings are slaughtered in three groups, one after another — the first group enters, the doors of the courtyard are locked, etc. As is found in Pesachim (64a)." "<b>Between the evenings.</b> From six hours and onward is called בֵּין הָעַרְבַּיִם, for the sun inclines toward its setting to grow dark; and the term <b>between the evenings</b> appears to me to mean those hours between the eveninging of the day and the eveninging of the night — the eveninging of the day at the beginning of the seventh hour, from when the evening shadows stretch out, and the eveninging of the night at the beginning of the night. עָרַב is a term of dusk and darkness, as in ״עָרְבָה כָּל שִׂמְחָה״ (Isaiah 24)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:38.542037676Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}